    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d213">

                <pb facs="0318" n="223" sameAs="frus:frus1936v04/pg223"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/8070</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 546</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-06-25">June 25, 1936</date>.<lb/>[Received July
                        28.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to enclose a copy of a
                        report<anchor n="47" xml:id="d213-f1"/> written by the Military Attaché,
                    Colonel Joseph W. Stilwell, with regard to Chinese military preparations along
                    the so-called Lung-hai line.</p>
                <p>Colonel Stilwell, who recently traveled along this line from Cheng-chow, the
                    intersection of the Lung-hai Railway and the Ping-han Railway, to the coast,
                    states that there is no evidence of military activity of any kind, that
                    conditions seem to indicate either that the Chinese have made elaborate
                    preparations and concealed them more skilfully than any other military power has
                    yet learned to do or that they have made none at all, that, if no preparation
                    has been made, General Chiang Kai-shek either has no intention of waging war
                    against the Japanese or believes he can wage war without preparations, and that
                    the office of the Military Attaché believes that General Chiang has <pb
                        facs="0319" n="224" xml:id="pg224"/> no intention whatever at present of
                    opposing the Japanese along the so-called Lung-hai line.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>For the Ambassador:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">George R.
                            Merrell, Jr.</hi></signed><lb/><hi rendition="#italic">First Secretary
                        of Embassy</hi></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
